<% provide(:title, 'Stdlib Line Probe Demo') %>
<h1>Stdlib Line Probe Demo</h1>

<p class="lead">
  Tests line probes on Ruby stdlib code loaded before DI started.
  Requires per-method iseq support (PR #5501).
</p>

<div class="card border-info">
  <div class="card-header"><h4 class="card-title">How to Test</h4></div>
  <div class="card-body">
    <ol>
      <li>In Datadog, set a <strong>line probe</strong> on one of these targets:</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="text-warning" style="margin-top: 10px;">
      Use the <strong>full absolute path</strong> below. Relative paths like <code>pathname.rb</code>
      may match vendored copies (e.g. bundler) and fail with MultiplePathsMatch.
    </p>
    <table class="table table-sm" style="margin-top: 5px; margin-bottom: 15px;">
      <thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>File (use this exact path)</th><th>Line</th><th>Coverage</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <% @probe_targets.each do |target| %>
          <tr>
            <td><%= target[:label] %></td>
            <td><code style="font-size: 0.85em;"><%= target[:path] %></code></td>
            <td><%= target[:line] %></td>
            <td>
              <% case target[:coverage] %>
              <% when :full %>
                <span class="badge text-bg-success">full</span>
              <% when :partial %>
                <span class="badge text-bg-warning">partial</span>
              <% when :partial_no_line %>
                <span class="badge text-bg-danger">partial (line not covered)</span>
              <% when :none %>
                <span class="badge text-bg-danger">not targetable</span>
              <% else %>
                <span class="badge text-bg-secondary">unknown</span>
              <% end %>
            </td>
            <td><%= target[:description] %></td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <ol start="2">
      <li>Wait for the probe to appear as active on <%= link_to "DI Status", di_status_path %></li>
      <li>Click the corresponding button below</li>
      <li>Check the snapshot in Datadog — you should see the method arguments and local variables</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="card card-body bg-light">
  <h4>Run the Demo</h4>
  <% @probe_targets.each_with_index do |target, i| %>
    <a href="/debugger_test/stdlib_probe_run?kind=<%= target[:kind] %>"
       class="di-demo-btn"
       <%= 'style="margin-left: 10px;"'.html_safe if i > 0 %>>
      <strong><%= target[:label] %></strong><br>
      <small><%= target[:description] %></small>
    </a>
  <% end %>

  <div id="di-demo-result" class="alert alert-info" style="margin-top: 15px;">Click a button above to invoke a stdlib method.</div>
</div>

<script>
  document.querySelectorAll('.di-demo-btn').forEach(function(btn) {
    btn.addEventListener('click', function(e) {
      e.preventDefault();
      var url = this.getAttribute('href');
      var label = this.querySelector('strong').textContent;
      var resultEl = document.getElementById('di-demo-result');

      resultEl.className = 'alert alert-info';
      resultEl.innerHTML = '<strong>' + label + ':</strong> Loading...';

      fetch(url)
        .then(function(res) {
          if (!res.ok) {
            return res.text().then(function(text) {
              throw new Error('HTTP ' + res.status + ': ' + text.substring(0, 200));
            });
          }
          return res.text();
        })
        .then(function(text) {
          resultEl.className = 'alert alert-success';
          resultEl.innerHTML = '<strong>' + label + ':</strong> ' + text;
        })
        .catch(function(err) {
          resultEl.className = 'alert alert-danger';
          resultEl.innerHTML = '<strong>Error:</strong> ' + err;
        });
    });
  });
</script>

<div class="card">
  <div class="card-header"><h4 class="card-title">Background</h4></div>
  <div class="card-body">
    <p>
      Most stdlib and gem code is loaded at boot time, before DI's code tracker starts.
      The whole-file iseqs for these files are garbage collected, but per-method iseqs survive
      because they're referenced by their class/module. DI's backfill collects these per-method
      iseqs and uses them to target line probes on specific methods.
    </p>
    <p>
      This works for any line that falls within a surviving method iseq. Lines in file-level
      setup code (outside any method) are not targetable because those iseqs don't survive GC.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
